abstract: >
  A governance model for how AI capabilities get built at a university, as
  distinct from how AI gets used. Structured as a model building code
  intended to be adopted with local amendments the way a jurisdiction
  adopts an I-Code. It borrows working mechanisms from construction
  governance: a statement of need before design, occupancy classification
  by objective trigger, plan review against the stated need, permits that
  carry conditions and expire, inspections at defined hold points before
  work is concealed, a certificate of occupancy distinct from completion,
  record drawings, and certification of reusable components so that
  consumers inherit a listing rather than re-entering review.

  Its central design commitment is that gates belong at points of
  irreversibility rather than at milestones, following published research
  finding that external heavyweight change approval correlates with worse
  delivery performance without reducing change failure rates. Human review
  is therefore reserved for questions no automated check can answer, and
  the model declines to document any control its target platforms do not
  actually enforce.
